name swim_cycle
describe Goal 236's second check: walk off the land into the sea, swim out, turn round, swim back and climb out, and do it under three different sea states. Run it as `--ramp wind-speed:0.5,3,8` -- wind and waves share one direction and one strength (make_wave_field), so the wind speed IS the sea state, and 0.5 / 3 / 8 m/s is glass / chop / swell. The stance sequence and the inside-solid count are what this asserts; the shore-pop assist is the piece most likely to break, because it was tuned against a flat surface.
backend both

option --walk
option --no-taa

# Ground along z=0 from tools/svo_render --xz: 4.88 at x=108, 1.16 at 111, -0.49 at 112,
# -9.09 at 116. Sea level is 0, so walking +X from 108 enters the water at about x=111.6.
pose_ground 108,0 -90 -6 1.7
hold forward 8
hold none 2
look 90 -6 1
hold forward 10
wait 3


# GOLDEN POLICY (measured, this scenario has none / only the early ones):
# A capture taken after sustained MOTION is not reproducible on this engine today. Two identical
# runs of the same scenario differ by 5.3% to 35.5% of pixels, because at speed the body outruns the
# LOD rebuild and which tree is resident when it stops decides what it sees. Captures taken AT REST
# reproduce to 0.0001-0.11%, well under the 1.5% gate. So the moving captures here are written for
# eyeballing and deliberately have no golden until Prompt 004 removes the rebuild storm.

capture end swim_cycle no-golden
assert frames > 0
assert contrast_percent > 6
assert inside_solid == 0
# Entering, floating and leaving the water is a handful of transitions; per-crest flicker in the
# surf would be dozens. See waterline_hold for the measurement that sets this scale.
assert stance_changes < 20
